<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sisebut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 621</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sisebut was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> from 612 to 621. He campaigned against the Byzantine province of Spania, taking Málaga and other coastal towns, and against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> and Asturians. A learned ruler who corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a>, he wrote a Life of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> and a poem on eclipses, and he ordered the forced baptism of the kingdom's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sisebut.jpg" alt="Sisebut" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Ex0pos at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sisebut1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sisebut ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, Gallaecia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a> from 612 until his death in February 621. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a> reports that before becoming king he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, a puzzling entry, since the region was already Visigothic under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Leovigild</a>. As king he campaigned successfully against the remnants of Byzantine power in Spania, strengthened Visigothic control over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> and Cantabrians, built friendly relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and reinforced the fleet that Leovigild had established. Nearly all the territory in the Iberian peninsula that the Eastern Roman emperor Justinian had seized was recovered, except the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>. He struck coins at Recopolis, as other Visigothic kings did, and his political practice recalled late Roman traditions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Sisebut was devoted to Chalcedonian Christianity. On his accession in 612 he forced his Jewish subjects to convert, and in 616 he ordered that those who refused be punished with the lash. The historian Herwig Wolfram places these measures within a broader tradition of imperial legislation that had the approval of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-fathers">Church Fathers</a>. He also consecrated churches, among them the martyrial basilica of St Leocadia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 618.</p><p>He was closely associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a> and is presented as the author of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poem on astronomy, the Carmen de Luna, dedicated to a friend identified as Isidore. The historian Chris Wickham compares him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> as a king with a reputation as a writer, though he adds that Sisebut was otherwise undistinguished apart from his persecution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. His son succeeded him as Reccared II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sisebut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nobles were taught reading, writing, science, medicine, law and philosophy, as the Institutionum disciplinae confirms; King <b>Sisebut</b> wrote poems, one on astronomy, and Isidore of Seville's Etymologies, an encyclopaedia of the age's knowledge, was read throughout medieval Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Leovigild retook inland areas, <b>Sisebut</b> captured Málaga around 615, and Suintila expelled the last imperial forces around 624.</blockquote><a href="/state/byzantine-spania">Byzantine Spania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Toledo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.87,-4.03&amp;year=611" aria-label="Toledo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4451 737 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sisebut"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4901 1007h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4901 1007h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.87,-4.03&amp;year=611">What was here in 611 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
